ORWELL'S VICTORY by Christopher Hitchens

2002. Penguin Books. Paperback.

The first political writer I took notice of was Christopher
Hitchens. He seems to have followed the path of his great friend
Martin Amis's dad Kingsley Amis in moving from the left in his youth
to the right in his adult years.

This book offers a re-assessment of Orwell in the light of what
Hitchens sees as misconceptions of him and his work. The book is well
divided into looking at Orwell from the facets of Empire, Left-wing,
Right-wing, America, Englishness and Feminism.

Orwell's legacy is in good hands. He was a very clear-headed writer;
awkward with women and perhaps a repressed homosexual, but right on
just about every issue. The out-of-focus cover photo of Orwell
looking thoughtful while enjoying a cigarette does the book no
favours. This could be the worst-presented Penguin book I've seen
since their sickly-green Day of the Triffids.

During my reading of this book, my sister made me aware of a modern
book suggesting Orwell's first wife Eileen played a huge part in his
writing. Google research shows me the book Wifedom: Mrs Orwell's
Invisible Life by Anna Funder (acclaimed author of Stasiland). I'm not
interested in Orwell studies enough to read this; it brings to mind
the movie "Hidden Figures" on African-American women in NASA; another
work of art I don't need to experience.

